  "title": "A direct hop whose gateway's NeighborInfo names another node with the byte is dropped",
  "rules": ["lnk_01", "lnk_02", "lnk_04", "lnk_05"],
  "reasoning": [
    "Casey's Q4 answer, (a) (PR #66 comment 6968763406, item 2), as PR #67 words it in lnk_01: \"a direct hop whose gateway's NeighborInfo resolves that low byte to a different node is dropped, and these drops are a data-quality count in the run report\". The README names that count lnk_01_direct_hop_contradicted.",
    "c48-g's one NeighborInfo report, at 23 08:00, lists c48-n1 (byte 65), c48-sb (66), c48-n2 (67) and c48-n3 (67). c48-g never heard c48-n1, c48-n2 or c48-n3 by radio.",
    "c48-sa (byte 65), receipts at 10:10, 10:20 and 10:30 with relay 65: a direct hop, so relay_node resolves to c48-sa, and NeighborInfo resolves to c48-n1. They differ: each receipt is dropped by lnk_01, and the three are the case's lnk_01_direct_hop_contradicted count, 3.",
    "c48-sb (byte 66), receipts at 10:15, 10:25 and 10:35 with relay 66: a direct hop, and the report lists exactly one neighbour with 66, c48-sb itself. They agree: link_source relay_node, all three counted, 3 packets, kept.",
    "c48-sc (byte 67), receipt at 10:40 with relay 67: a direct hop; the report lists two neighbours with 67, so NeighborInfo resolves nothing and the direct hop stands alone: link_source relay_node, counted. 1 packet: dropped by lnk_04.",
    "c48-sa, c48-sb and c48-sc are stationary on c20 and c48-g on g16 (lnk_02), so c48-sa's receipts fail lnk_01 alone. The counted receipts pass lnk_05 at 2745.603 m. c48-sb's hour: SNR 5, 6 and 7 dB give 6.0; RSSI -90, -95 and -100 dBm give -95.0; position_uncertainty_m 386.484807 m; neither end is precise."
  ],
